A||N||8||Xtreme Fiero's||Engine balance job, do I need it?||||5||
Z||000000||Xtreme Fiero's||10-10-2000||10:49 PM||||<BR>Well so far I have decided on geting a Stock crank Magnafluxed and whatevered and the same for the rods for my duke build up I will also be geting the fiero store 84 Performance build kit W/ TRW hyper pistons. will my motor need to be balanced? the motor will NEVER pass 7000rpm i'll keep it below 6,500 for sure. What does the balance job do. I thought is only raised redline and made the car rev up faster and It sounds expensive, is it?<P>This has been recomended by the guy doing my crank/rod work to be done localy by another shop. I mean he knows what he is talking about but has anyone else had thier motor balanced for a rebuild I never heard if this step being nessary before. only a high dollar option.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||10-10-2000||11:10 PM||||The better an engine is balanced, the longer it will last, the smoother it will run and might have a little better performance. Any time you deviate (sp?) from the factory parts, the balance will be off- over bore, different rod bolts, different casting on the crank etc. . .  For a long, happy life- get the rotating assembly balanced: be sure to include the flywheel and balancer hub when getting the balance job done.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Xtreme Fiero's||10-10-2000||11:30 PM||||<BR>Cooter: I just got an E-mail form Joe, my Crank/rod dude  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> and what  you tow say makes sense I also thught it was going to be BIG bucks but it dosen't seem to bad he said around $100. so I figure $150 here in MD. is that about right?<P>I cant wait  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I hope to have the car back on the road by Jan. 01||||reg||1
Z||000003||webbee||10-11-2000||12:05 AM||||Balancing should be done to any engine you expect to keep. It makes it run so smooth.<BR>Don't hesitate to spend the money, especially if you are modifing.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||10-11-2000||08:18 PM||||Cooter is totally right about what a balance job will do for you. While it is not necessary to balance your engine if you have the engine balanced your engine will last longer, run smoother, and maybe make a little more power. It is necessary though to balance the engine if you do not use your original crankshaft and connecting rods. A balance job dose not increase the engines redline. A stronger crankshaft, connecting rods, and main & rod bolts with lighter pistons increase your engines reline along with a stronger/lighter valve train  (springs) and matching cam, intake manifold and exhaust. A balance job in the New England area will run anywhere from $150-$250. It is costing me $175 for my 3.4-liter Camaro engine. I think it is cheap insurance and well worth the investment.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ThatRickGuy||10-11-2000||11:04 PM||||Balance assemble was $130 on my 2.8-3.1 conversion. a good balance job is your 5500 warenty. If you want to spin 7000 out of the engine, invest in knife edging, it's a lot more expencive, but it'll be less likely to gernade on you.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Xtreme Fiero's||10-11-2000||11:20 PM||||what is "knife edgeing"? what do they do there and how much more will is cost? I will be useing a 84-86 stock ECM, the rev limiter there will keep in down below 5000 rpm so will I still need the motor "knife edged"?  <P>P.S. The car will/is for sale so I don't want to Throw money into it but I do want I to be a Great motor just not 10,000 rpm great. I want to sell the car for less than $2000 with the motor I'm already up to $900 in parts I have yet to get the block work done or the build kit orderd. all I got comeing right now are the Crank, Head, Rods, Intake & TBI.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ThatRickGuy||10-12-2000||01:20 PM||||I'm not sure on the exact process of knife edging. I beleive it has to do with hardening the crank, balancing, and making the edges perfectly smooth and level. you shouldn't need it at all, nife edging is more of a professional super high reving thing, but if you're planing on running an engine beyond 7000 rpm... Then again, how many street cars get run past 7k on purpose?!? (If anyone says 'mine does' then, I'd love to see you drive threw down town Washington DC traffic!!!) if the rev limiter is set to 5000, you could get away with not getting a balance job, but it's highly suggested that you do, even just a tiny vibration can lead to the engine littlerally tearing itself apart (I saw the remains of a corvet 454 that had done that...) And well, after just getting my engine back, $1600 would be a lot to lose just because I didn't spend the extra $130 on a balance job.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||10-12-2000||02:49 PM||||Knife edging refers to reshaping the crankshaft's leading edges. If you examine a stock crankshaft, you'll notice that the edges on the between the main and crank jornals are squared off. When a crankshaft has been "knifed" it simply means that these edges have been knife edged (and of course had the crankshaft re-balanced). The theory is that as the crank rotates, the knife edge will cut through the oil and air with less resistance which will free up HP. While this sounds really trick, it only adds HP in the high RPM range. <P>I've read some testing about this and the article basically said that you'll gain just about as much HP by using a simple crank scraper. (I know, I know... what the heck is a crank scraper???). A crank scraper is simply a piece of sheet metal that conforms to the rotation profile of the crankshaft. Simple put, as your crankshaft rotates, the scraper wipes off (or scrapes) the excess oil being splashed by the rotating assembly. This reduces the amount oil being splashed and, again, frees up HP.<P>Boiling it down... Knifing a crank = Big $$$, Crank scraper = little $$$. Same result.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||4
